Revision history of I18N-CodeSets


0.00_00  2026-11-13  RC
    + RdKw::I18N::CodeSets: the code sets of stdin, stdout, stderr,
      argv, env and filesys (by default, as Encode::Locale says),
      and the diag mode (unicode or ascii), from new or from -C
      specs (parse), and the process's own (locale).
    + initstd, to_fs, un_fs (as in RdKw::WwW::GenGenLib 0.01_00,
      but of lists, and with { prog => ... } for who could not),
      un_argv, to_argv, decode_argv, encode_argv, getenv, setenv,
      unsetenv: in and out of the process, or an error (FB_CROAK,
      unless { check => ... } says otherwise; decode_argv and
      encode_argv take it as Encode::Locale's do).
    + diag_name, diag: names for people, quoted to show what would
      not (according to the diag mode).
    + $RdKw::I18N::CodeSets::LOCALE is what locale says, and may be
      local()ized.
    + new of an object: a copy of it, with what it is told applied;
      parse is of an object only, which it sets (CLASS->new->parse
      for a new one), or leaves as it was if it dies.  parse splits
      specs by commas, and takes an encoding alone for all of the
      categories (the pseudo-category all); 0 is short for ascii,
      and 1 for UTF-8 (or unicode), as for SITECONF_CODESETS.
    + The encoding locale unsets a category, so that parse('locale')
      resets everything to what new gives; the locale is looked up
      when a category is used, not when it is set.
    + stderr set to ASCII (by any of its names) makes diag follow it,
      and so ascii.
    + diag escapes what stderr cannot spell (\x{...}), and diag_name
      under unicode quotes it so.
    + parse_from_env(NAME, prog => ...) and parse_from_argv(\@ARGV,
      prog => ...): parse of what an environment variable says, and
      of the -C (or -Cglued) that @ARGV starts with, which it takes
      off, saying how many; applied to the object itself.
    + RdKw::I18N::CodeSets::Functions: diag, diag_name, to_fs, un_fs,
      to_argv, un_argv, encode_argv, decode_argv, getenv, setenv,
      unsetenv, initstd, and reinitstd, as functions (of locale),
      for export on request (:all for all of them).
